---
title: Quality Education Infrastructure
order: 5
icon: book-open
---

Eighty children cram into a classroom built for forty. Science labs sit empty — no equipment, no chemicals. Pit latrines overflow in the rain. This is the daily reality for Nairobi's public schoolchildren, and it is a betrayal of their constitutional right to education.

We will launch a county-wide school infrastructure rehabilitation programme targeting the most urgent needs first: new classrooms where overcrowding is worst, modern science and computer laboratories, libraries stocked with current materials, and gender-sensitive sanitation blocks with running water. Every school will be accessible to learners with disabilities. We will establish a Schools Infrastructure Fund financed by a dedicated 15 percent of the county development budget, with projects prioritised by a committee of education professionals and parent representatives.

For our brightest students from disadvantaged backgrounds, we will create a transparent, merit-based county scholarship programme covering tuition, books, and living expenses through secondary school and university. The selection process will be published online — every awardee named, every school disclosed. An independent county scholarship board, free of political interference, will administer the programme. No scholarship will be a reward for loyalty. Every scholarship will be an investment in potential.

Nairobi's future depends on a skilled workforce. We will build a vocational training centre in every sub-county offering accredited courses in information technology, electrical installation, plumbing, automotive engineering, hospitality, and the creative arts — each one partnered with industry to guarantee graduates are employable. We will also launch a county adult education programme, giving every Nairobian who missed formal schooling a second chance to earn a basic certificate. Education is the surest path out of poverty — and this county government will pave it.
